en tested are slowing perhaps of the  growth in the growth rate why do we  think that might be is it because of the  behavioral changes that many many people  have made  given statements from yourself and the  prime minister or is it just perhaps  different rates of spread between  different groups so i think there's a  number of different points there number  one i'm not sure i would agree that the  growth rate that we have strong evidence  it has come down it's around two two  days for uh doubling time  for most regions across the country and  it is very high in london still  so i think we shouldn't accept that as  you've noticed uh noted that people do  have different behaviors around their  testing over festive periods and so for  the next few days we think the data will  likely not give a particularly true  picture it's likely to come back in on  track as we go past the festive season  um so i think that those are the the key  key points in relation to  of course we are expecting perhaps uh to  hear something in terms of additional  restrictions perhaps as early as next  week from the government uh one item  from from the sage minutes which have  just been released which which led to my  attention is this and i'm giving this to  you site on scene so apologies dr harris  the minute says this data will be  unreliable over the festive period as  testing and hospital admission patterns  change this will make it difficult to  interpret any apparent trends over the  next few days do you believe that the  prime minister the chief medical advisor  chief scientific officer  will have the evidence to be able to  make a decision early next week on  further restrictions  there's probably three pieces of of  evidence and they are being looked at on  a daily basis and we will obviously be  continuing to provide that uh  information over christmas so i think  that that is a certainty we we keep  looking at the data all of the time um  number one is it's actually the ons  survey the community infection survey  which is run routinely which  systematically samples people across the  country and we thank all of them for  their help  that gives us the background infection  numbers now not all of those people who  have infections in the community  sometimes because they don't know  they're asymptomatic they won't always  come forward so there is always a  difference and it goes back to your 200  000 number if we actually look at what  ons uh is saying at the moment there's  somewhere around uh in the previous week  uh they estimate 1.2 to 1.5 million  people had coronavirus doing during that  week now those are not the numbers that  come through through our testing so we  always know that there is a difference  there but i think the third point is  actually looking at what we're really  concerned about we're concerned about  people's health we're concerned about  transmission but most of all we're  concerned to make sure that our health  services can keep functioning and of  course the health service data continues  to come back in on a daily basis and  again that is looked at regularly so i  think the short answer is yes we have  the data good um just in terms though um  uh doctor something you mentioned a  moment ago something you alluded to a a  couple of moments ago that doesn't seem  to be within uh the hospital within our  hospitals at the moment the pressures on  intensive care and on on ventilators  that you might have expected is that  just a concomitant of that which we were  discussing before that you know the  majority of people going in are slightly  younger at the moment so that's one of  the uncertainties you're absolutely  right it's relatively early days there's  always a lag between the rising  community cases and of course this has  happened so quickly that we're still  literally only four weeks since we came  across this new variant and but we will  we do anticipate more cases will come in  as cases appear  in the older age groups but we are  seeing signals across other countries  and we monitor that routinely that  suggests that perhaps the length of stay  in hospital is a little bit less people  are not needing oxygen quite so much and  perhaps not going through to  intensive care the only caveat i would  put on that is of course we had  relatively high  case rates before we found omicron  and our hospitals are trying to deal  valiantly as they always do with routine  winter pressures so i think uh there is  what pressure is the nhs under and then  what contribution is omicron making and  it's both of those together that we need  to take into account  um dodge harris of course you're a lot  closer to those making the the political  decisions and all this i barely get a  card from an mp these days um so you may  well disagree with what i'm about to  suggest but it certainly feels like from  the government's perspective  hospitalizations  remains the key data point in terms of  further restrictions but given what we  are seeing particularly in the health  service in london significant numbers of  staff themselves are are off with  omicron it feels like you know staffing  might well become a tipping point for  further restrictions  so staffing for the health service for  the care service i'd like to just you  know of course yes that's a balance as  well for all our carers out there and  actually for many of our other critical  infrastructure services you will want to  ensure you have some food on your table  for your christmas meal tomorrow and  subsequently um and of course what we  have done is looked at the evidence  around the isolation period and what is  safe and we now have uh lateral flow  test new rapid antigen tests available  uh this year that we didn't have last  year and so we've been able to safely  reduce the isolation period down as long  as people are taking and have two  negative uh lateral flow devices 24  hours apart so that actually starts to  address some of the 