r booster um obviously as i say  jcbr we'll keep a look on that and we  will look at the vaccine effectiveness  and our hospital data on a daily basis  as we go forward and publish in due  course  i don't always i do hope you could not  get any presence to wrap because i'm  going to hold on to you for another  couple of minutes we've just had a  little bit of interesting data coming  from up from the ons and we were talking  about this just a second ago and they  estimate 1.7 million people in the uk  had covered 19 in the week up to  december the 19th the highest number  since compatible figures back began back  in autumn 2020. i i suppose is that  within the bulk of the park of what you  would have expected given the  transmissibility of omicron  so i think what we're seeing is very  very high growth rates and and you're  absolutely right i think uh the data  translates through something like 1 in  35  people across england have covered and  in london it's something like a  staggering one in 15. this is very  uh you know we haven't seen rates like  this before but it is predominantly  still an infection of younger people so  the 20 to 29 year group particularly  we've still got some delta cases in very  young children and we have  delta cases in those people mostly who  are currently in hospital so this is a  very very mixed picture  and as we've just been discussing some  of the critical things  in in managing this is to be absolutely  sure  what the severity risk is and our data  at the moment is still quite early i  mean i think it gives me an opportunity  just to reinforce on those figures  exactly that when people are enjoying  their christmases and we really want  them to that we don't let omicron enjoy  it as well that they should be  being careful about what they're doing  obviously  doing face hands space ventilate the  rooms that they're in and particularly  if you're going to visit somebody who is  vulnerable in any way or has  immunosuppression  to do 